<p>A South Korean court in Seoul delivered its long-awaited verdict in the insurrection/rebellion case against former president Yoon Suk Yeol, a case stemming from his short-lived attempt to impose martial law in December 2024. The South China Morning Post reported that the court found Yoon guilty of insurrection. The trial has been a major test of South Korea’s democratic guardrails after what coverage described as one of the country’s most serious constitutional crises in decades.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/_arze7CNNddUciYPKTJr5vwvkYwgt02WOEhYOQTZYr9RELD6aK4V6sWgrfQZFJu2ry2bgyErPfe0gKZQw3vRloXyU6XsUx8mRxJnXseWNgW79cNf52xd52BW9ToI2Wxj0-FvuLu9qsgJYKyBCmY-GFoAgGFIxKuRi-4nXbOZcJqqqZvmwOPIKESvSPChAVnlPt1k-4s-t4bWfw5wLio5Lw' alt='Yoon Suk Yeol in Seoul in 2022.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>At court: ABC News reported that Yoon arrived at a Seoul court ahead of the verdict on rebellion charges tied to the martial-law attempt.</li><li>What happened: The BBC published an explainer on the martial-law decree, the protests that followed, and the road to Yoon’s insurrection trial.</li><li>Legal ripple effects: The Financial Times described the insurrection ruling as the most consequential of several cases triggered by the failed martial-law declaration.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Three-judge panel at Seoul Central District Court: A three-judge panel convicted Yoon Suk Yeol of insurrection over the martial-law decree, in what SCMP described as the harshest and most consequential ruling yet tied to the December 3, 2024 episode. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/week-asia/people/article/3343989/south-koreas-ex-president-yoon-found-guilty-insurrection'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Yoon supporters: Supporters kept backing Yoon and rallied in Seoul even after the martial-law move failed, a sign that the legal process has not erased his political base. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/02/19/world/yoon-korea-martial-law-president/yoons-martial-law-declaration-failed-but-his-supporters-stayed-loyal'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>The Economist (analysis): The Economist argued that a verdict would not resolve what it called Yoon’s toxic political legacy, and that the country will keep grappling with the fallout well beyond the courtroom. (<a href='https://www.economist.com/asia/2026/02/18/south-korea-is-still-haunted-by-its-disgraced-ex-president'>The Economist</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/ef281426-e417-4675-9f5c-6d8bdaabb987'>South Korea’s ex-president Yoon faces verdict over failed martial law</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.economist.com/asia/2026/02/18/south-korea-is-still-haunted-by-its-disgraced-ex-president'>South Korea is still haunted by its disgraced ex-president</a> - economist.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/former-south-korean-president-yoon-faces-verdict-rebellion-130290209'>Former South Korean President Yoon faces verdict on rebellion charges</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/videos/c70kw0nrxreo'>Martial law, protests and a president tried for insurrection: How did South Korea get here?</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/02/19/world/yoon-korea-martial-law-president/yoons-martial-law-declaration-failed-but-his-supporters-stayed-loyal'>Yoon’s martial law declaration failed, but his supporters stayed loyal.</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/week-asia/people/article/3343989/south-koreas-ex-president-yoon-found-guilty-insurrection'>South Korea’s ex-president Yoon found guilty of insurrection</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/south-korean-court-weighs-death-sentence-for-ex-president-yoon-over-insurrection-charges-13981401.html'>South Korean court weighs death sentence for ex-president Yoon over insurrection charges</a> - firstpost.com</li></ul>